    summary: "On June 17, 1775, the American colonial militia and British Regulars fought what became one of the most consequential early engagements of the American Revolutionary War — the Battle of Bunker Hill, fought largely on the adjacent Breed\'s Hill overlooking Boston Harbour in Massachusetts. Though technically a British victory, the battle proved to be a profound psychological and strategic turning point. Colonial forces, poorly equipped and largely untrained, held their fortified position against two full frontal assaults by crack British infantry before finally being driven back on the third charge when their ammunition was exhausted. British casualties were staggering: over a thousand men killed or wounded, including a disproportionate number of officers, out of an attacking force of roughly 2,400.\n\nThe battle shattered the comfortable British assumption that colonial resistance would collapse at the first serious military pressure. General Thomas Gage, the British commander in Boston, wrote to London that the rebels had shown 'a conduct and spirit against us they never showed against the French.' The famous order — almost certainly apocryphal in its precise wording but real in its tactical logic — to 'don\'t fire until you see the whites of their eyes' reflected the colonial commanders' acute awareness that every musket ball had to count. Colonial losses were roughly half those of the British, and among the dead was the charismatic General Joseph Warren, one of the patriot cause\'s most gifted leaders, killed in the final retreat.\n\nThe ripple effects of Bunker Hill ran through the entire subsequent course of the war. It persuaded the Continental Congress to commit fully to a standing army under George Washington, who took command just weeks later. It convinced the British government that the rebellion was a serious military enterprise requiring substantial reinforcement rather than a police action against a rabble. And it gave the colonial cause something invaluable at the moment it needed it most: the evidence that ordinary American farmers and tradesmen could stand in open battle against the finest professional army in the world and make them bleed for every inch of ground.",
